{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqi7rypye1d","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"eager judge timeout keeps edge provisional not pruned","summary":"SPEC IS INCOMPLETE: production's eager-judge NEVER prunes an autowire candidate edge on a judge timeout. Two cases (daemon.js:1640-1646, routes/overlay.js:218-223, lib/headless-drain.js): (1) per-call claude -p timeout — the SIGKILL'd drain simply leaves those candidates unjudged, so the NEXT drain re-judges them (production RETRIES via re-drain; headless-drain also backs off on 429/overload). (2) terminal judgingTimeoutMs — the task falls back to ready and the surviving unjudged edges are KEPT as provisional: surfaced in the task projection, read as not-yet-verified context, never dropped. Therefore a faithful bench ON-arm must NOT default-prune on a claude -p cap-kill. It must (a) bounded-retry the judge call, (b) on terminal timeout keep-as-provisional (edge stays in get_task_context/answer context, flagged), (c) COUNT+log every timeout-kill so a run with 0 timeouts is provably faithful and a run with >0 can re-judge before trusting the score. The current SDK arm (judge.py _deterministic_fallback) default-PRUNES on cap-kill, which under-retrieves vs production and understates Zonoid's score.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-17T15:16:44.038Z","vec":[-0.06537546217441559,0.06691369414329529,-0.055291786789894104,-0.017192522063851357,0.05859121307730675,-0.012885349802672863,0.0011964719742536545,0.08497066050767899,0.026225049048662186,0.05878809094429016,-0.00031921081244945526,0.06276225298643112,-0.004630859009921551,-0.006580039393156767,-0.07166049629449844,0.012599784880876541,0.0796937346458435,-0.050081171095371246,-0.049041956663131714,0.0023492579348385334,0.026012828573584557,-0.03784925863146782,0.0500137023627758,-0.02048630826175213,-0.023274105042219162,-0.10165298730134964,-0.08143317699432373,-0.03471947833895683,0.014794934540987015,-0.06621244549751282,0.00624857796356082,0.024267585948109627